Apple allegedly pressured a component supplier to withdraw a public statement distancing itself from the display cracking issue affecting some iPhone Duo units, according to <em><a href="https://www.thelec.kr/news/articleView.html?idxno=64923">The Elec</a></em>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/iphone-duo-screen-crack.jpg" alt="" width="1205" height="677" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1072561" /><br/>
The allegation surrounds the little-known <a href="https://www.finemtec.com/eng/">Fine M-Tec</a>, which reportedly supplies internal metal plates supporting the iPhone Duo's folding screen. Last month, the Korean-based supplier published a statement denying that defects in its components were responsible for the reported display failures. <br/>
<br/>
<em>The Elec</em> reports that Fine M-Tec posted "Our official position on the customer's new product quality issue" on its website on October 26. The statement read: "The quality issues that are known to have occurred in some products recently are not caused by quality defects in the parts we supply." Fine M-Tec said it published the statement to counter accusations associated with suspected short sellers. It also warned of possible legal action against those spreading unsubstantiated claims.<br/>
<br/>
This statement, which did not outright name Apple or the iPhone Duo, appeared shortly after the first cases of hinge-related screen damage on Apple's first foldable began to emerge online.<br/>
<br/>
By November 4, however, the statement had disappeared from Fine M-Tec's website. Shortly afterwards, Korean publication <em><a href="https://dealsite.co.kr/articles/172308">DealSite</a></em> reported that its industry sources suggested the supplier had been pressured by Apple to remove it.<br/>
<br/>
In an editorial <a href="https://www.thelec.kr/news/articleView.html?idxno=64923">published Tuesday</a>, <em>The Elec</em> journalist Kim Geon said a Fine M-Tec representative had since contacted him requesting the removal of his <a href="https://www.thelec.kr/news/articleView.html?idxno=64235">earlier article</a> about the withdrawn statement. According to the representative's account, Apple had objected to the supplier publicly denying responsibility for the failures.<br/>
<br/>
